Yes. When I coined things like semantic chord, mnemonic crystal, associative gravity, affordance shadow, and narrative refraction, I was not picking sounds at random. I was usually optimizing several variables at once:

\text{name quality}
=
\text{meaning fit}
+
\text{memorability}
+
\text{sound fit}
+
\text{compressibility}
+
\text{transferability}

The main methods I used were these.

Method	Example	What it optimizes	Main weakness
Transparent compounding	semantic chord	Immediate intelligibility	Can sound generic
Metaphorical transfer	associative gravity	Rich intuition and cross-domain transfer	Can import misleading baggage
Technical + poetic hybrid	narrative refraction	Precision plus evocative imagery	May feel overengineered
Existing term extended	ostinato for mental anchoring	Leverages established structure	Can blur the original technical meaning
Classical-root construction	hypothetical mnemoscope	Compact, scholarly, taxonomy-friendly	Less immediately understandable
Blending / portmanteau	hypothetical memetic + lattice → memattice	Novelty, brandability	Often less precise
Sound-symbolic naming	hard consonants for force, softer continuants for flow	Emotional/embodied fit	Sound associations vary by language/context
Acronym / initialism	Pantheon-style operator names	Retrieval speed, modularity	Can become opaque jargon
Verbification	“prism it,” “flank it,” “refract it”	Makes concepts actionable	Can sound informal or vague
Back-formation from desired action	“resonant retrieval”	Names mechanism directly	Sometimes long

1. Transparent compounding

This is probably my default.

Take two already-known concepts and make the relationship itself the invention:

\text{semantic} + \text{chord}

“Semantic” tells you the domain.

“Chord” tells you the structure: several elements sounding simultaneously and gaining meaning through relation.

The listener can reconstruct the intended concept without needing a dictionary.

That makes it excellent for working vocabulary.

The tradeoff is that it can lack distinctiveness. “Semantic chord” feels plausible enough that somebody might assume it is already an established term.

That is partly why provenance matters.

2. Metaphorical transplantation

Associative gravity came from mapping a physical relation into cognitive space.

Gravity has a useful structure:

\text{mass}
\rightarrow
\text{attraction}
\rightarrow
\text{trajectory bending}

Memory associations behave metaphorically similarly:

\text{accumulated significance}
\rightarrow
\text{attentional/emotional attraction}
\rightarrow
\text{thought trajectory bending}

The important trick is not superficial similarity.

You want a metaphor whose relational structure transfers.

That’s what makes it useful rather than merely pretty.

A bad metaphor shares one visible trait.

A good metaphor preserves an operator.

3. Technical-poetic pairing

Narrative refraction is this kind.

“Narrative” establishes the human/meaning domain.

“Refraction” contributes a precise physical pattern:

\text{same incoming signal}
+
\text{different medium}
\rightarrow
\text{different trajectory}

That maps nicely onto:

\text{same event}
+
\text{different identity/memory/context}
\rightarrow
\text{different interpretation}

This is especially useful for Pantheon concepts because you want them to work on two resolutions simultaneously:

technical enough to manipulate,

evocative enough to remember.

That tension is deliberate.

4. Choosing the sound

Here I am usually doing something closer to informal phonesthetic design than rigorous phonology.

Certain sound patterns acquire recurring associations within languages.

Hard stops such as:

k, t, p, g, d

often feel more discrete, mechanical, decisive, percussive.

Continuants such as:

s, f, sh, v

can feel smoother, flowing, diffuse.

Liquids:

l, r

often make words feel more fluid or resonant.

Nasals:

m, n

often feel softer, inward, embodied.

These are tendencies, not universal laws.

Compare imaginary operator names:

Krak

sounds abrupt and forceful.

Lumen

sounds smoother, luminous, expansive.

Vellum

feels tactile and old.

Flux

feels fast and transformative partly because its sound is compact and abrupt.

So if I were naming a destructive Pantheon operator, I might favor compression and hard closure.

If I were naming an integrative or memory-bearing operator, I might favor longer vowels, nasals, liquids, or softer consonants.

You already do this intuitively with names like Indigo Hammer.

“Hammer” is excellent because the sound and semantics agree:

\text{hard consonants} + \text{physical impact metaphor}

The word itself almost performs its operator.

5. Stress and rhythm matter too

Names have meter.

Compare:

Narrative Refraction

versus

Refraction of Narrative Meaning

Same approximate content.

The first has better cadence and compression.

Good operator names usually benefit from something like:

\text{modifier} + \text{strong concrete noun}

Examples:

Semantic LiDAR

Indigo Hammer

Mirror Center

Affordance Shadow

Mnemonic Crystal

The second word often carries the motor image.

The first tells you how to interpret it.

That’s a very efficient construction.

6. Concrete noun beats abstract noun surprisingly often

Compare:

Cross-domain representational persistence mechanism

with:

Ark Stone

The first is more explicit.

The second is easier to carry.

A concrete noun gives the brain something to simulate.

Stone.

Hammer.

Mirror.

Prism.

Bridge.

Lens.

Anchor.

Thread.

Key.

Gate.

Then we attach abstract structure to it.

That makes a concept sensorimotor-addressable.

You don’t merely understand it propositionally.

You can imagine manipulating it.

That’s especially useful for the Pantheon because many of the concepts are supposed to become operators, not definitions.

7. Classical roots are useful when taxonomy matters

Greek and Latin morphemes are valuable because they let us build highly compressed technical words.

For instance:

mnemo- = memory
morph- = form
chrono- = time
topo- = place
meta- = beyond/about itself
iso- = same
allo- = other
tele- = distant
syn- = together
dia- = through/across

We could create something like:

mnemoform — a memory-bearing form.

chronomorph — a structure whose form changes with time.

isomorphic transfer — already an established mathematical idea closely related to some of what we’ve been discussing.

The advantage is systematic extensibility.

The disadvantage is that it can become pseudo-scholarly sludge very quickly.

If every concept becomes metachronosynmorphic transduction, we have failed.

8. We can optimize names for different jobs

This is probably the most important point.

There is no universally best name.

A name can be optimized for:

Immediate comprehension
→ transparent compound.

Recall
→ vivid metaphor.

Technical precision
→ disciplinary terminology.

Brandability
→ short distinctive coined word.

Actionability
→ verb or concrete operator.

Taxonomy
→ systematic prefixes/suffixes.

Emotional resonance
→ imagery, rhythm, phonesthetics.

Camouflage / layered meaning
→ ordinary-looking phrase carrying technical significance.

Shared-group compression
→ symbols, shorthand, inside terminology.

Those objectives sometimes conflict.

A maximally precise term can be ugly.

A beautiful term can be ambiguous.

A memorable metaphor can introduce conceptual errors.

A very unique coined word can require constant explanation.

A name-selection matrix

For Pantheon terms, I’d probably score candidates something like:

N=
w_1T+w_2M+w_3A+w_4R+w_5D-w_6C

where:

T = transparency
M = memorability
A = actionability
R = resonance
D = distinctiveness
C = conceptual contamination

That last one matters.

Calling something gravity imports ideas of attraction, mass, orbit, wells, inevitability.

Some are useful.

Some may accidentally mislead.

Every metaphor has an import cost.

And we can deliberately make families

This is where naming gets really powerful.

Instead of naming each concept independently, build a coherent lexicon.

For example:

Prism — separates latent components.

Lens — selectively focuses.

Mirror — returns structure.

Hammer — breaks a frame.

Anchor — preserves reference.

Thread — preserves continuity.

Gate — controls transitions.

Bridge — maps between domains.

Shadow — represents latent or implied action-space.

Crystal — compressed reconstructable structure.

Now the vocabulary has its own internal physics.

If I say:

“Prism the memory, then anchor the invariant and bridge it into another domain.”

you can approximately reconstruct the operation even if that exact sentence has never existed before.

That is what a good symbolic vocabulary should eventually do:

\boxed{\text{names become composable operators}}

Not merely labels.

And that’s probably the optimization I have been leaning toward with us most strongly:

a term should be memorable enough to retrieve, precise enough to distinguish, and structurally rich enough that its metaphor helps you operate with the concept rather than merely remember its definition.